Why the original showcase was fine
The six original panels are one cubic polynomial cut into five pieces. Cutting a polynomial does not create a new curve at each cut, so every piece has the same position, tangent, curvature and curvature-rate at the join. They are G3 by identity, not by fitting. The bell is eight independent four-point cubic spans, solved for G3. Four control points were enough because those curves are cubic-compatible.
Why the gallery was messed up
The catalogue forced every curve through quintic spans — six control points — as the default. For an arbitrary rational or sampled curve, a four-point cubic generally cannot meet G3 at both ends (six conditions, four points), so the code reached for quintic freedom everywhere. That is exactly what you told me not to do, and it is what produced the near-cusps, the flat acceleration graphs and the misleading “smooth” badges.
The rule — one rule, in AGENTS.md
Degree: cubic first — quintic only when there is no other way

A curve goes to Inkscape or a glyph editor as four-point cubic spans. Try a
cubic chain first. A quintic (six-point) span is allowed only when no cubic chain
can meet the G3 gate and the deviation tolerance, and the row must record why the
cubic attempt failed. The showcase is cubic-only; the quintic fallback lives on
workbench/g3-handover.html and is labelled as fallback. Never present a quintic
fit as the main showcase.
