The user says "best would be simple cubic beziers that are collapse n=5 beziers". Interpretation: they want simple cubic Béziers; the n=5 Béziers should collapse to cubics. I.e., prefer cubic spans, and if the 5 Bézier spans can collapse to cubics, use simple cubics. So the showcase should be built from simple cubic Béziers, where the five spans collapse to cubics. But G3 with cubics generally fails. However, there are special cases where cubic spans are G3:
- A single cubic polynomial chopped into spans: same polynomial → all derivatives match → G3. This works for any cubic polynomial curve, because the pieces are exact segments of the same cubic. The ogee and lattice wave are such.
- A cubic Bézier chain can be G3 if the joins are at points where the cubic's third derivative matches and parameterization is linear; effectively the spans are pieces of a single cubic.
