I'm here. No — and the distinction is exactly the one the Pell page draws.
A p-triple gives rational numbers: a² + b² = c² means a/c and b/c are Fractions. So p-triples give every rotation whose sine and cosine are both rational — 3-4-5 (36.87°), 5-12-13, and so on. √3 is irrational, so no p-triple produces it. 60° has sin 60° = √3/2, which is not a ratio of integers, so 60° is not a p-triple angle, and an equilateral triangle has no realisation with rational coordinates at all: rotate a rational vector by 60° and the √3 appears in both components.
What you can get is √3 as a limit, which is precisely Pell:
x² − 3y² = 1  →  x/y = 2/1, 7/4, 26/15, 97/56, …  → √3
That is rational approximation, never equality. So a true Reuleaux triangle can be drawn to any visible accuracy, but it cannot satisfy this project's exactness rule ("exact means Fraction, asserted integral before it is drawn"). It belongs beside Gen-Arch and the catenary as declared irrational, not in the exact set.
Two honest routes if you want it in the inventory:
1. Fitted constant width — build it from three arcs with centres placed at 60° using the best Pell rational (26/15), measure the width variation (it will be small and stateable in percent), and label it fitted, not exact. The method is the same as the Levien rail: report the error rather than hide it.
2. Exact constant width, different construction — a circle is trivially exact; and arc-built constant-width curves with rational centres exist in principle if the centre polygon is equidiagonal with rational coordinates (the Reuleaux triangle specifically needs the equilateral one). That is a genuine open check, not something I would assume — it needs a construction proved before it is drawn.
So the corrected statement is: a true Reuleaux needs √3, and √3 is irrational, so it is fitted or declared — p-triples do not buy it, Pell only approaches it. If you want, I'll add option 1 as a properly labelled fitted curve with its measured width variation.

