Why it matters here (the paper's result)
The radial of a curve is the locus of its centres of curvature, re-plotted as polar points: angle = the normal angle, radius = ρ = 1/κ. Fig. GA0 draws the clothoid with three osculating circles on the left, and on the right those same radii as polar distances — which trace a lituus. That is the whole idea, without an evolute drawn in place.
The theorem: the radial of a log-aesthetic curve is Gen-Arch with c = α − 1, where α is the LCH slope. Measured on the page for α = −2, −1, 2, 3: fitted exponents match 1/(α−1) and the largest deviation over 241 samples is 2.2e-16.
The three dials — and my earlier mistake
α — the paper's LA parameter: κ ∝ s^(−1/α).
p — this repo's LAC dial: κ ∝ s^p. Therefore p = −1/α.
c — Gen-Arch: c = α − 1 = −1/p − 1.
So the card Fig. C70 · LAC: p = 3 is α = −1/3, not α = 3. α = 3 would be p = −1/3, whose radial is the Fermat spiral (c = 2). I corrected the stem-cut labels to p = 3, which is the repo's dial and cannot be confused with α or with the p-triple's a. Special cases fall out: clothoid (p = 1, α = −1) → c = −2 lituus; circle involute (p = −1/2, α = 2) → c = 1 Archimedean; log spiral (α = 1) is the exception, c = 0 being only the limit.
