PH = Pythagorean Hodograph.
- Hodograph = the derivative curve: plot (x′(t), y′(t)) instead of (x(t), y(t)).
- Pythagorean = at every t, those two derivative components plus a third polynomial form a Pythagorean triple:
x′(t)² + y′(t)² = σ(t)²
So a PH curve is a curve whose velocity obeys the Pythagorean theorem exactly, with σ a polynomial rather than a square root.
The name comes from the construction: feed two polynomials u(t), v(t) through Euclid's triple formula,
x′ = u² − v²,   y′ = 2uv,   σ = u² + v²
and (u²−v², 2uv, u²+v²) is a triple for every t. Example, u=t, v=1: x′=t²−1, y′=2t, σ=t²+1.
What it buys: speed |r′| = |σ| is a polynomial, so arc length is polynomial and the unit tangent/normal are rational — hence exact offsets. What it costs: only a restricted family of curves, and integer coordinates/arc lengths only after a scale.


